---
import Layout from '@/components/Layout.astro';
import { getGuide, guideJsonLd } from '@/lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('/guides/cron-cheat-sheet');
---

<Layout
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  path={guide.path}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[guideJsonLd(guide)]}
  narrow
>
  <article class="prose-tool max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[rgb(var(--text))] sm:text-3xl">
      {guide.title}
    </h1>

    <p>
      Cron is the Unix scheduler that runs commands on a repeating timetable. A crontab line is five
      fields followed by the command, and almost every cron mistake comes from misreading one of those
      five fields. Here is the whole syntax, the schedules worth memorising, and the point where you
      should reach for a systemd timer instead.
    </p>

    <h2>The five fields</h2>
    <p>
      Fields are separated by spaces, in this order. A sixth field can be prepended on systems that
      support seconds (Quartz-style schedulers and some crontab implementations).
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Field</th>
            <th scope="col">Range</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>minute</td>
            <td>0–59</td>
            <td>The first field, not the hour.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>hour</td>
            <td>0–23</td>
            <td>24-hour clock, in the server's time zone.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>day of month</td>
            <td>1–31</td>
            <td>Days that do not exist in a month are skipped.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>month</td>
            <td>1–12 or JAN–DEC</td>
            <td>Names are case-insensitive.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>day of week</td>
            <td>0–7 or SUN–SAT</td>
            <td>0 and 7 both mean Sunday.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Operators</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>*</code> — every value in the field, e.g. <code>* * * * *</code> is every minute.</li>
      <li><code>5</code> — one value.</li>
      <li><code>1-5</code> — a range, inclusive.</li>
      <li><code>*/15</code> — a step; <code>*/15</code> in the minute field means every 15 minutes.</li>
      <li><code>10-40/10</code> — a range with a step.</li>
      <li><code>1,15</code> — a list; combine any of the above with commas.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The day-of-month/day-of-week quirk</h2>
    <p>
      When <em>both</em> day fields are restricted, classic cron treats them as an OR, not an AND.
      <code>0 3 13 * FRI</code> runs on the 13th of the month <em>and</em> every Friday, not only on
      Friday the 13th. If you need that AND, put the check in the command itself or use a scheduler
      with a clearer expression language.
    </p>

    <h2>How it differs from a systemd timer</h2>
    <p>
      Both run a unit on a schedule, but they are not the same tool. Cron is a daemon reading crontab
      files, one line per job, with no dependency awareness and no catch-up. Systemd timers are an
      ordinary unit pair: a <code>.timer</code> that says when, and a <code>.service</code> that says
      what.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Aspect</th>
            <th scope="col">cron</th>
            <th scope="col">systemd timer</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Calendar syntax</td>
            <td><code>*/5 * * * *</code></td>
            <td><code>OnCalendar=*:0/5</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Interval timers</td>
            <td>Achieved by a step field</td>
            <td><code>OnUnitActiveSec=30min</code> — monotonic, independent of wall-clock jumps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Missed runs (machine asleep)</td>
            <td>Lost</td>
            <td><code>Persistent=true</code> runs once on the next boot</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Logging</td>
            <td>Mails output, or you redirect it yourself</td>
            <td>Captured in the journal by default</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dependencies</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td><code>After=</code>, <code>Requires=</code>, full unit ordering</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Random jitter</td>
            <td>Manual</td>
            <td><code>RandomizedDelaySec=</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Schedules worth memorising</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Expression</th>
            <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>* * * * *</code></td>
            <td>Every minute</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>*/5 * * * *</code></td>
            <td>Every 5 minutes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>0 * * * *</code></td>
            <td>At the top of every hour</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>30 3 * * *</code></td>
            <td>Daily at 03:30</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>0 9-17 * * MON-FRI</code></td>
            <td>On the hour, 09:00–17:00, weekdays</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>15 2 * * 0</code></td>
            <td>Sunday at 02:15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>0 0 1 * *</code></td>
            <td>Midnight on the first of each month</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>0 4 1,15 * *</code></td>
            <td>04:00 on the 1st and 15th</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Shorthand keywords</h2>
    <p>
      Most crontabs accept a handful of words that expand to common schedules:{' '}
      <code>@reboot</code> (once at startup), <code>@hourly</code>, <code>@daily</code> (or{' '}
      <code>@midnight</code>), <code>@weekly</code>, <code>@monthly</code> and{' '}
      <code>@yearly</code>. They are convenient and readable, though not part of the original POSIX
      specification, so check that your cron implementation supports them.
    </p>

    <h2>Time zones matter more than people expect</h2>
    <p>
      The clock cron uses is the local time of the machine, not UTC, unless you say otherwise.
      <code>0 3 * * *</code> therefore runs at 03:00 in the server's zone and shifts by an hour when
      daylight saving changes — and a job scheduled inside a skipped hour simply does not run that
      day. For anything that must be predictable, pin the zone explicitly (many crontabs accept a{' '}
      <code>CRON_TZ=</code> or <code>TZ=</code> line, and systemd timers accept a zone in the
      calendar expression) and prefer UTC on servers.
    </p>

    <h2>Check an expression before you ship it</h2>
    <p>
      A scheduler that fires at the wrong instant is worse than one that never fires, because you only
      find out from the damage. The <a href="/tools/cron-parser">cron expression parser</a> turns an
      expression into plain English and lists its next run times in UTC, so a misread field shows up
      as an obviously wrong timestamp. If you would rather build the expression from a form, the
      <a href="/tools/crontab-generator">crontab generator</a> assembles it for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Assuming the hour comes first. It is minutes, then hours — <code>30 8</code> is 08:30.</li>
      <li>
        Forgetting that cron gets a minimal environment: your <code>PATH</code>, aliases and shell
        profile are not loaded, so use absolute paths and set variables in the crontab itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        Expecting a working directory. <code>cd /path &amp;&amp; ./job.sh</code> is safer than hoping.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dropping output on the floor and losing errors, or the opposite — letting cron mail the output
        of a chatty job. Redirect it deliberately.
      </li>
      <li>
        Forgetting that <code>%</code> is special inside a crontab: an unescaped percent becomes a
        newline and is fed to the command's standard input, so escape it as <code>\%</code> when you
        mean the character.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>More guides</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/base64-explained">Base64 explained</a> — what Base64 is and where it belongs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/which-hash">Which hash should I use?</a> — digests, passwords and HMAC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/color-contrast-wcag">Colour contrast and WCAG</a> — accessible colour
        choices.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>